counterfactuals.output:
A plant for producing a synthesis gas product from a hydrocarbonaceous feed gas comprises a reforming section, a synthesis gas cooling and separation section, and a selective recycle system configured to vary the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio of the synthesis gas product. The plant is particularly suitable for processing natural gas, methane-containing gas, refinery off-gas, biogas, associated gas, or other light hydrocarbonaceous feed streams.

In one embodiment, the plant comprises:

1. **a feed preparation section** configured to receive a hydrocarbonaceous feed gas and to remove contaminants therefrom, optionally including sulfur compounds, chlorine compounds, heavy hydrocarbons, particulates, and catalyst poisons;

2. **a reforming section** comprising at least one synthesis gas generation reactor selected from the group consisting of a steam reformer, an autothermal reformer, a catalytic partial oxidation reactor, a non-catalytic partial oxidation reactor, a gas-heated reformer, a pre-reformer, and combinations thereof;

3. **an oxidant and/or steam supply system** configured to introduce oxygen, oxygen-enriched gas, air, steam, carbon dioxide, or combinations thereof into the reforming section;

4. **a cooling section** configured to cool an effluent gas from the reforming section and to recover heat therefrom, optionally by generation of steam;

5. **a gas separation section** configured to separate at least a portion of the cooled synthesis gas into two or more separated gas fractions, including at least one hydrogen-enriched fraction and at least one carbon monoxide-enriched fraction; and

6. **a recycle control system** configured selectively to recycle at least a portion of one or more of the separated gas fractions to an upstream location in the plant, thereby increasing or lowering the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio of the synthesis gas product.

The hydrocarbonaceous feed gas may comprise methane as a principal component. The feed preparation section may include a hydrodesulfurization unit, an adsorption bed, a mercury removal unit, a chloride guard bed, a preheating heat exchanger, and/or a saturator. The reforming section may be operated under conditions effective to convert at least a portion of the hydrocarbonaceous feed gas into a raw synthesis gas comprising hydrogen, carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, water, methane, and optionally nitrogen and argon.

The gas separation section may comprise one or more of a pressure swing adsorption unit, a membrane separation unit, a cryogenic separation unit, a partial condensation unit, an absorption unit, a wash column, or combinations thereof. In one preferred arrangement, the gas separation section is configured to provide a hydrogen-enriched gas fraction, a carbon monoxide-enriched gas fraction, and a residual gas fraction comprising methane, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, carbon monoxide, or combinations thereof.

In operation, a hydrocarbonaceous feed gas is introduced into the feed preparation section to produce a purified feed gas. The purified feed gas is combined with steam, carbon dioxide, oxygen, oxygen-enriched gas, or mixtures thereof and passed to the reforming section. The reforming section produces a raw synthesis gas. The raw synthesis gas is cooled, optionally shifted in whole or in part, and then passed to the gas separation section. At least a portion of the raw synthesis gas, or a gas derived therefrom, is separated into a hydrogen-enriched fraction and a carbon monoxide-enriched fraction.

The hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide ratio of the final synthesis gas product is adjusted by selectively recycling separated gas fractions. When an increased hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio is desired, at least a portion of a carbon monoxide-enriched fraction may be withdrawn from the synthesis gas product stream or recycled to the reforming section under conditions wherein carbon monoxide is converted or consumed, thereby reducing the relative carbon monoxide content of the product. Additionally or alternatively, at least a portion of a hydrogen-enriched fraction may be added directly to the synthesis gas product stream, or recycled to a location downstream of the reforming section and upstream of the product outlet, thereby increasing the hydrogen content of the product.

When a lowered hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio is desired, at least a portion of the hydrogen-enriched fraction may be recycled to the reforming section, used as fuel, purged, or otherwise removed from the product stream, thereby decreasing the relative hydrogen content of the synthesis gas product. Additionally or alternatively, at least a portion of the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction may be added directly to the synthesis gas product stream or recycled to a location downstream of the reforming section, thereby increasing the carbon monoxide content of the product.

In certain embodiments, the plant is configured such that the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction is recycled to an inlet of the reforming section, to a pre-reformer, to an autothermal reformer, to a partial oxidation reactor, or to a feed mixing zone upstream thereof. Recycling the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction to a high-temperature reforming zone may affect the synthesis gas composition by participation in equilibrium reactions, including the water-gas shift reaction, reverse water-gas shift reaction, steam reforming reactions, dry reforming reactions, and oxidation reactions. In further embodiments, a carbon dioxide-enriched fraction is recycled to the reforming section to lower the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide ratio by promoting dry reforming or reverse water-gas shift chemistry.

In other embodiments, the hydrogen-enriched fraction is recycled to a burner, radiant box, fired heater, oxygen-fired reformer, autothermal reformer, or other fuel-consuming component of the plant. Such use reduces or limits hydrogen content in the synthesis gas product while recovering fuel value from the separated hydrogen. Alternatively, the hydrogen-enriched fraction may be compressed and introduced into the product synthesis gas stream to increase the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide ratio.

The selective recycle system may comprise control valves, compressors, analyzers, flow controllers, and a process controller. The process controller may receive a measured or calculated hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio of the synthesis gas product and may regulate the flow of the hydrogen-enriched fraction, carbon monoxide-enriched fraction, carbon dioxide-enriched fraction, methane-containing fraction, residual gas fraction, or combinations thereof, to one or more recycle destinations. The recycle destinations may include the feed gas line, the steam reformer inlet, the autothermal reformer inlet, the partial oxidation reactor inlet, the pre-reformer inlet, a water-gas shift reactor inlet, a product blending line, a fuel system, or a purge line.

A process for producing synthesis gas from a hydrocarbonaceous feed gas may comprise the steps of:

(a) providing a hydrocarbonaceous feed gas;

(b) treating the hydrocarbonaceous feed gas to remove catalyst poisons and produce a purified feed gas;

(c) reacting the purified feed gas with steam, oxygen, carbon dioxide, or mixtures thereof in a reforming section to produce a raw synthesis gas comprising hydrogen and carbon monoxide;

(d) cooling the raw synthesis gas;

(e) separating at least a portion of the cooled raw synthesis gas into a hydrogen-enriched fraction and a carbon monoxide-enriched fraction;

(f) forming a synthesis gas product from at least a portion of the raw synthesis gas, the hydrogen-enriched fraction, the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction, or combinations thereof; and

(g) adjusting the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio of the synthesis gas product by selectively recycling at least a portion of the hydrogen-enriched fraction or the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction.

In one mode of operation, the process includes increasing the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide ratio by directing at least a portion of the hydrogen-enriched fraction to the synthesis gas product and reducing, purging, combusting, or reforming at least a portion of the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction. In another mode of operation, the process includes lowering the hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide ratio by directing at least a portion of the carbon monoxide-enriched fraction to the synthesis gas product and reducing, purging, combusting, or recycling to an upstream reaction zone at least a portion of the hydrogen-enriched fraction.

The plant and process permit flexible production of synthesis gas having a selected hydrogen-to-carbon monoxide molar ratio, for example a ratio suitable for methanol synthesis, Fischer-Tropsch synthesis, oxo synthesis, acetic acid production, ammonia co-production, hydrogen production, or carbon monoxide production. The ratio may be controlled continuously or intermittently, and may be adjusted without substantial alteration of the primary reforming operating conditions. The selective recycling of separated gas fractions thereby provides a controllable and efficient means for matching synthesis gas composition to downstream process requirements.
